<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Evidence Recall under Different Token Budget</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we further present the curve of evidence recall metric at different retrieval context length settings (from 2k to 4k). The results are shown in <a href="#A1.F1" title="Figure A1 ‣ A.2 Evidence Recall under Different Token Budget ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ HiChunk: Evaluating and Enhancing Retrieval-Augmented Generation with Hierarchical Chunking" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure A1</span></a>. Compared with other chunking methods, the HC200+AM method always maintains the best performance.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.11552v1/my_plot_er.svg" id="A1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:471/157;" width="471" height="157"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure A1</span>: </span><span id="A1.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Evidence recall metric across different token budget on HiCBench(<math id="A1.F1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>).</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
